Z||000003||maryjane||09-10-2001||10:56 PM||||I'd say a 10 (1 being easiest-10=impossible),<BR>Ya see, I'm way down here in Texas and you're way up there in Canada, so ....Oh you probably meant how hard for you do do it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> sorry.<P>Well you will probably need a wheel puller, you have to remove the calipher, need some lube for putting the brake parts back together, an impact will help to get the nut off the hub easily, but not a neccesity. BUT, I'm not sure if you can change just the bearing, may have to change the hub/bearing as an assembly. Someone else with a lot better answer will be along shortly. Good luck<BR>See, I told ya some with a lot better answer would come along. They came along while I was one-finger peckin this out.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||Leadfoot||09-10-2001||11:14 PM||||Aren't you guys forgetting the rear seal?  To replace the seal don't you need to disconnect the lower ball joint from the knuckle?  That is how I did it......all three times.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||09-11-2001||12:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Leadfoot:<BR><B>Aren't you guys forgetting the rear seal?  To replace the seal don't you need to disconnect the lower ball joint from the knuckle?  That is how I did it......all three times.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Leadfoot,<P>Only if the axel is going to be removed, which in this case, that is not necessary.<P>This is a simple thing to do and the only thing not common in most tool boxes is a 30mm socket for the axel nut.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||gt boy||09-11-2001||12:50 AM||||well resto guy...i can say that 30mm sockets are as common as racoon sh@t in my tool box.  you see i am a motorcycle fanatic, but my hips and legs can't seem to take it any more that is why i have 3 gts now...i guess that would mean that i am a fiero fanatic now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>------------------<BR>"it's too bad he had to run out of talent half way through the corner..."||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||09-11-2001||07:45 AM||||The most difficult part is getting the damn axle nut loose.<BR>It's easier to do with a wheel mounted, and the car on the ground. (Otherwise, the opposite side wheel will try to turn, unless your parking brake is REAL good.)<P>The shop manuals say to use a puller to remove the bearing/hub assembly. (It *is* replaced as an assembly only, BTW), but I don't know anyone who's ever needed one.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||gt boy||09-11-2001||11:29 PM||||so it sounds like i need a puller...would a 3 jaw do?<BR><P>------------------<BR>"it's too bad he had to run out of talent half way through the corner..."||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||09-17-2001||02:20 AM||||From the replies to your question, I would have to say that this "job" your talkin' about BLOWS!! Hope I don't have to do anything like that soon<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||09-17-2001||06:15 AM||||Only ever done work on the 88s, but it's cake.<P>PS: any NAPA auto parts should have a 30mm deep impact socket.  I wouldn't even THINK of tackling that nut withough an impact gun btw...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||09-17-2001||08:33 PM||||Its cake on 88's if you can find that parts for them. lol<P>I had a huge prob a while back. My hub/bearings were so badly shot in the front right I couldn't even drive my car. I was about to sell it then I found someone on the forum (thanks mr mike) who sold me a unit.<P>------------------<BR>88 Fiero Coupe...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mesopotamis_1.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000012||snafus87||09-17-2001||09:24 PM||||If you need a hub assembly, if you choose to go that route, I can probably get you one for US $90 plus shipping, though it would take about 1 week to get it for you and the shipping time.  I can't imagine you would want to wait that long.<P>1.  Loosen lugs and remove wheel<BR>2.  using either a T47 (what I used) or a T50 Torx bit, remove the caliper bolts and caliper and support it somewhere in the wheel well<BR>3.  Loosen and, if you can, remove the 3 19mm bolts that hold the dust shield and hub assembly on<BR>4.  Using a 30mm socket (preferrably impact for torque hold up), remove the axle nut.<BR>5.  Remove the entire hub assembly<BR>6.  reverse for install.<P><BR>Up to you if you want to try the bearing, or just go with the whole hub assembly.<P>------------------<BR>JT Krupa<BR>85 GT<BR>(fastback conversion)<BR>180+ K miles<BR>Lehighton, PA||||reg||1
Z||000013||snafus87||09-17-2001||09:26 PM||||BTW,<P>It took me a little over an hour.<P>I would rate it around a 3, maybe up to 6 depending on how much trouble you have with the caliper bolts...mine were a son of a ...<P>Recommend the use of penetrating oil...helps significantly<P>Oh, and watch the knuckles (speaking from experience, ouch....golly gee willakers that hurt!!)<P>------------------<BR>JT Krupa<BR>85 GT<BR>(fastback conversion)<BR>180+ K miles<BR>Lehighton, PA||||reg||10
